{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1220 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "99a4e5212f59f2eb835ca90435eeec819a1d120a0df51602f9c2770c65d95a32" :edb/db-before-t 1219 :edb/db-after-t 1220 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534532 50 #inst "2026-10-07T20:44:51.312Z" 13194139534532 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293437 13194139534532 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293451 13194139534532 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293444 13194139534532 true] [17592186152717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534532 true] [17592186152717 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186152717 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-78680.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)$?" 13194139534532 false] [17592186152717 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186152724 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534532 true] [17592186152724 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/4e9218dcfc77ec080139f2650ea19f78199557e0ce2d8d53c1996ec0ea6145a6.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186152724 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-79278.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?" 13194139534532 false] [17592186152724 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/dfbe843a9ad0847d7174984fa7c7c48834af48b3ee2335b5383b15f7fb826763.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293438 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1089 "q-106405" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1113 #uuid "8c5260bf-7853-55b2-aacc-c274f42999cd" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293437 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293439 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293440 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293441 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293442 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293443 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1041 "selection" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293442 13194139534532 true] [17592186293438 1110 #uuid "23997d42-5193-5915-a21f-f7d0b34cc6eb" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293439 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293439 1111 #uuid "4beb4dd7-21e3-5447-b949-ef6f23b5e291" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293440 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293440 1111 #uuid "1c99d03c-1ff4-5a10-adaa-70bcc44e772d" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293441 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293441 1111 #uuid "338acf0c-7949-50e9-a083-803a4948a091" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293442 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293442 1111 #uuid "17f69769-9fb8-5130-95f3-bb7a3aad5ee6" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293443 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293443 1111 #uuid "37c3f0c6-95bb-5f64-b213-7c57150e4518" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293445 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1089 "q-106711" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/8498bb189b51954a79f8ca6638631d6acd0a3d4fe86f6f4cecb2de77ee370f94.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1113 #uuid "0cf0095e-d6fa-5b1f-a653-5f34a9b15d59" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293444 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/fb686f7295c2c6ac3058361ecc0438ae56d650950833185b17ec959d654cc511.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293446 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293447 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293448 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293449 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293450 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1041 "selection" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293446 13194139534532 true] [17592186293445 1110 #uuid "12de4955-fbe4-57bb-a8cd-8909d0c71bd6" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293446 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293446 1111 #uuid "ab3fff1d-bba7-5567-8e2d-0456fcfc32c3" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293447 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293447 1111 #uuid "7bb6ef26-028d-53f6-ac25-4c6bc207c487" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293448 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293448 1111 #uuid "25d07480-81c6-57f5-9de6-7fdf5e35d843" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293449 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293449 1111 #uuid "0556d804-2df7-54b1-a3e2-3b477163afce" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293450 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293450 1111 #uuid "a238209b-2298-5203-a208-adf6280b4df2" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293452 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1089 "q-327811" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1113 #uuid "280bf9fd-56f0-5bf3-9058-1fd2e618f75c" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293451 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293452 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534532 true] [17592186293452 1041 "field-1" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293452 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293452 1110 #uuid "2653bdba-d523-561b-85e9-b0a8fd2aa90a" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293453 1034 "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" 13194139534532 true] [17592186293453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534532 true] [17592186293453 1111 #uuid "69db830a-a2f2-59e1-854b-c32b1481bec4" 13194139534532 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-106405" 17592186293437 "q-106405/selection" 17592186293438 "q-106405/selection/27c01cee2b8d8bb4789a55d08f0be55c997a8966705fdb6f696d5bc4f3a79981" 17592186293439 "q-106405/selection/44eec51842eb0b7e7239ffb06e8f6c704d029dd9c1a6845c69ced6dc014eb6e5" 17592186293440 "q-106405/selection/856ca9109e385c69e379e0a27d3435c5152ff0f12ae503a530a85a4b90d0620b" 17592186293441 "q-106405/selection/aa3d12c7f02db6b7752ed86dd0a10eb4fa674b5fb28e9b94b68fa8e1c99c4b51" 17592186293442 "q-106405/selection/abe8d5c14bfb53aed1ff074bfc7e0140cb313dc27798cb43c430dd09535e706c" 17592186293443 "q-106711" 17592186293444 "q-106711/selection" 17592186293445 "q-106711/selection/20f2278fd4e56315448eddfaa845501a5a92a8375fe4a2d33888bc35070c54c1" 17592186293446 "q-106711/selection/2